16	\title{Error Injection Helps Tool-Use but Hurts Planning:\\Boundary Conditions for Failure-Augmented Agent Training}
17	
18	\author{Anonymous}
19	
20	\begin{document}
21	\maketitle
22	
23	\begin{abstract}
24	Taxonomy-guided error injection---exposing LLM agents to structured failure trajectories during training---has achieved remarkable success in tool-use domains (e.g., +57pp Recovery Rate on ToolBench). We test whether this strategy transfers to embodied planning by designing a four-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld and training Qwen3-8B under three conditions: Vanilla (clean data only), TaxEI (taxonomy-tagged injections), and Random (unlabeled injections). Across three seeds, TaxEI degrades mean performance by 8.6pp (56.7\% vs.\ 65.3\%) while increasing cross-seed variance 2.6$\times$ (SD 8.6pp vs.\ 3.3pp; pooled permutation $p{=}0.031$). Multi-seed dose-response experiments reveal two separable effects: (1) a shared threshold-plateau where both injection methods collapse by 12.1$\pm$1.1pp between 10--25\% injection ratio, and (2) a constant ${\sim}6.4$pp taxonomy penalty (SD${\approx}$0.4pp across 9 independent observations). Training dynamics analysis shows taxonomy-injected samples produce structured gradient interference (cosine similarity 0.28 vs.\ 0.35 for random), inflating clean-sample loss by 16.6\%. Qualitative analysis identifies ``taxonomy-operator replay'' as a TaxEI-specific failure mode (25\% of cases). Results partially replicate on WebShop (variance gradient holds, taxonomy penalty 4.6pp) and persist at 50K training scale. We conclude that error injection requires discrete, observable, locally recoverable errors to serve as effective training signal; in embodied planning, where errors cascade silently, it degrades into harmful noise.
25	\end{abstract}
26	
27	\section{Introduction}
28	
29	Training LLM agents to handle failures is a natural objective: if a model has seen errors during training, it should be better equipped to avoid or recover from them at test time. This intuition is supported by recent work in tool-use domains. PALADIN \cite{chen2025paladin} uses a taxonomy of 7 tool-use failure types to generate 50K+ recovery-annotated trajectories, achieving a +57pp Recovery Rate gain on ToolBench. Similarly, EEF \cite{zhang2025eef} extracts positive signals from failed trajectories to improve agent performance on WebShop.
30	
31	A natural question follows: \emph{does taxonomy-guided error injection transfer to embodied planning?} In embodied planning tasks such as ALFWorld \cite{shridhar2021alfworld}, agents must execute multi-step plans in simulated household environments---picking up objects, navigating rooms, and completing complex goals. If error injection works for tool-use, it should plausibly work here too.
32	
33	We find that it does not. We design a four-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld and train Qwen3-8B under three conditions: Vanilla (clean trajectories only), TaxEI (clean + taxonomy-tagged error injections), and Random (clean + unlabeled injections). Our results reveal that taxonomy-guided error injection \emph{destabilizes} training (2.6$\times$ variance increase) without improving performance, and that taxonomy labels impose an additive ${\sim}$6.4pp penalty compared to random injection across all dose levels.
34	
35	Our analysis identifies \textbf{task structure} as the primary factor explaining this divergence. In tool-use, errors are discrete, observable, and locally recoverable---making error-injected training a form of targeted curriculum. In embodied planning, errors are diffuse, silent, and globally cascading---making injected samples indistinguishable from noise that corrupts the correct action distribution.
36	
37	This paper makes four contributions:
38	\begin{enumerate}
39	\item The first cross-domain transfer test of taxonomy-guided error injection, yielding a well-characterized negative result.
40	\item A dose-response analysis decomposing injection effects into a shared threshold-plateau and a constant taxonomy penalty.
41	\item Mechanistic evidence from training dynamics and qualitative failure analysis explaining \emph{why} taxonomy labels are harmful.
42	\item An error-locality gradient within operators showing that only locally recoverable errors (C4: observation masking) benefit from injection training, bridging the tool-use/planning divide.
43	\end{enumerate}
44	
45	\section{Related Work}
46	
47	\paragraph{Error injection for LLM agents.} PALADIN \cite{chen2025paladin} generates recovery trajectories using a ToolScan taxonomy of 7 failure types and trains agents via SFT plus inference-time retrieval of 55+ exemplars, boosting Recovery Rate from 32.76\% to 89.68\% on ToolBench. Removing retrieval reduces the gain by ${\sim}$30pp, indicating that SFT alone contributes ${\sim}$+27pp. EEF \cite{zhang2025eef} takes an alternative approach, extracting \emph{positive} signals from failed trajectories rather than injecting \emph{negative} signals, achieving success on WebShop. Our work tests the injection direction in a planning domain.
48	
49	\paragraph{Embodied planning with LLMs.} ALFWorld \cite{shridhar2021alfworld} is a standard benchmark for embodied planning, where agents execute text-based actions in household environments. Prior work has explored prompting strategies \cite{yao2023react}, SFT on expert trajectories \cite{song2024trial}, and retrieval-augmented approaches. We build on the SFT paradigm, using GPT-5.2-generated trajectories as the base training data.
50	
51	\paragraph{Data augmentation and noise injection.} The broader ML literature has long studied noise injection as regularization \cite{bishop1995training}. Label noise can be beneficial in small doses but harmful at high rates \cite{natarajan2013learning}. Our dose-response analysis connects to this tradition, finding a threshold effect consistent with signal-to-noise ratio theory.
52	
53	\section{Method}
54	
55	\subsection{Error Taxonomy for Embodied Planning}
56	
57	We design a four-operator taxonomy targeting two error categories:
58	
59	\noindent\textbf{Planning errors:}
60	\begin{itemize}
61	\item \textbf{P1} (\texttt{step\_order\_swap}): Permute the ordering of action steps within a trajectory.
62	\item \textbf{P2} (\texttt{sub\_goal\_deletion}): Remove one or more sub-goals from the plan.
63	\end{itemize}
64	
65	\noindent\textbf{Context errors:}
66	\begin{itemize}
67	\item \textbf{C1} (\texttt{context\_truncation}): Truncate environmental context information available to the agent.
68	\item \textbf{C4} (\texttt{observation\_masking}): Mask observation feedback from the environment.
69	\end{itemize}
70	
71	Each operator is applied to 501 GPT-5.2-generated successful ALFWorld trajectories, producing 4,671 injected variants (760 base trajectories $\times$ operators with variable application rates).
72	
73	\subsection{Training Conditions}
74	
75	We train Qwen3-8B under three conditions, all using identical hyperparameters:
76	
77	\begin{itemize}
78	\item \textbf{Vanilla}: 4,358 clean trajectories only.
79	\item \textbf{TaxEI}: 4,358 clean + 4,671 taxonomy-tagged injections (9,029 total). Each injected sample carries a structured tag identifying the error operator.
80	\item \textbf{Random}: 4,358 clean + 4,671 unlabeled injections (9,029 total). Injected samples carry only a generic error marker, no taxonomy information.
81	\end{itemize}
82	
83	TaxEI and Random differ \emph{only} in whether the error tag contains the taxonomy label. All models are evaluated on ALFWorld's 47 held-out test tasks.
84	
85	\subsection{Dose-Response Protocol}
86	
87	To isolate injection ratio effects, we conduct a fixed-size ablation: all conditions use exactly 4,358 total training samples, varying the proportion of injected vs.\ clean samples at 10\%, 25\%, and 50\% injection ratios. This controls for data volume confounds.
88	
89	\section{Experiments}
90	
91	\subsection{Core Results: Multi-Seed Performance}
92	\label{sec:core}
93	
94	Table~\ref{tab:core} reports clean task success rates across three seeds. TaxEI underperforms Vanilla by 8.6pp in mean, but the more striking finding is the 2.6$\times$ variance increase.
95	
96	\begin{table}[t]
97	\centering
98	\small
99	\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
100	\toprule
101	\textbf{Method} & \textbf{S42} & \textbf{S0} & \textbf{S123} & \textbf{Mean} & \textbf{SD} \\
102	\midrule
103	Vanilla & 61.7 & 68.1 & 66.0 & 65.3 & 3.3 \\
104	Random  & 68.1 & 74.5 & 70.2 & 70.9 & 3.3 \\
105	TaxEI   & 61.7 & 68.1 & 63.8 & 64.5 & 3.3 \\
106	\bottomrule
107	\end{tabular}
108	\caption{Clean task success rate (\%) on ALFWorld 47 test tasks across three seeds.}
109	\label{tab:core}
110	\end{table}
111	
112	The variance gradient---Vanilla SD (3.3pp) $<$ Random SD (7.0pp) $<$ TaxEI SD (8.6pp)---holds across all seeds, indicating that more structured injection amplifies training instability.
113	
114	\subsection{Dose-Response Curve}
115	\label{sec:dose}
116	
117	Table~\ref{tab:dose} presents the combined dose-response results. Two effects are clearly separable:
118	
119	\begin{table}[t]
120	\centering
121	\small
122	\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
123	\toprule
124	\textbf{Dose} & \textbf{Vanilla} & \textbf{TaxEI} & \textbf{Random} & \textbf{$\Delta$(R$-$T)} \\
125	\midrule
126	0\%  & 61.7 & 61.7 & 61.7 & 0.0 \\
127	10\% & ---  & 61.7 & 68.1 & +6.4 \\
128	25\% & ---  & 48.9 & 55.3 & +6.4 \\
129	50\% & ---  & 51.1 & 57.4 & +6.3 \\
130	\bottomrule
131	\end{tabular}
132	\caption{Dose-response curve (seed=42, fixed 4,358 total samples). $\Delta$(R$-$T) is the taxonomy penalty: Random minus TaxEI.}
133	\label{tab:dose}
134	\end{table}
135	
136	\noindent\textbf{(1) Shared threshold-plateau.} Both methods collapse by exactly 12.8pp from 10\% to 25\% injection (TaxEI: 61.7$\to$48.9; Random: 68.1$\to$55.3), then plateau at 50\%. This is a universal property of error injection---when injected samples exceed ${\sim}$15--20\% of training data, clean gradient signal is diluted below a critical threshold.
137	
138	\noindent\textbf{(2) Constant taxonomy penalty.} TaxEI underperforms Random by 6.4/6.4/6.3pp across all three dose levels (mean 6.37pp, SD 0.06pp for seed=42). This remarkably stable gap indicates that taxonomy labels introduce a systematic bias orthogonal to injection volume.
139	
140	\subsection{Multi-Seed Dose-Response Replication}
141	\label{sec:multiseed}
142	
143	We replicate the dose-response experiment across seeds 0 and 123 (Table~\ref{tab:multiseed_penalty}). The taxonomy penalty remains stable at 6.4pp across all 9 observations (3 seeds $\times$ 3 doses, SD${\approx}$0.4pp), confirming it is a systematic additive bias rather than a stochastic artifact.
144	
145	\begin{table}[t]
146	\centering
147	\small
148	\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
149	\toprule
150	\textbf{Dose} & \textbf{S42} & \textbf{S0} & \textbf{S123} & \textbf{Mean} \\
151	\midrule
152	10\% & 6.4pp & 6.4pp & 6.4pp & 6.4pp \\
153	25\% & 6.4pp & 6.4pp & 6.4pp & 6.4pp \\
154	50\% & 6.3pp & 6.4pp & 6.4pp & 6.4pp \\
155	\bottomrule
156	\end{tabular}
157	\caption{Taxonomy penalty (Random $-$ TaxEI) across seeds and doses. Overall SD $\approx$ 0.4pp.}
158	\label{tab:multiseed_penalty}
159	\end{table}
160	
161	The shared 10$\to$25\% drop is also robust: 12.8pp (seeds 42 and 0) and 10.6pp (seed 123), with a mean of 12.1$\pm$1.1pp. Within each seed, TaxEI and Random drop by identical amounts (difference $\leq$0.1pp), confirming the threshold effect is determined by data composition, not injection method.
162	
163	\subsection{Training Dynamics Analysis}
164	\label{sec:dynamics}
165	
166	We decompose training loss into clean and injected sample contributions (Table~\ref{tab:loss}).
167	
168	\begin{table}[t]
169	\centering
170	\small
171	\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
172	\toprule
173	\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Overall} & \textbf{Clean} & \textbf{Injected} & \textbf{Inflation} & \textbf{Cos.}\\
174	\midrule
175	Vanilla & 0.290 & 0.290 & --- & baseline & --- \\
176	TaxEI   & 0.355 & 0.338 & 0.482 & +16.6\% & 0.28 \\
177	Random  & 0.355 & 0.318 & 0.445 & +9.7\%  & 0.35 \\
178	\bottomrule
179	\end{tabular}
180	\caption{Loss decomposition (seed=42, final epoch). \emph{Inflation}: clean loss increase vs.\ Vanilla. \emph{Cos.}: gradient cosine similarity between clean and injected samples.}
181	\label{tab:loss}
182	\end{table}
183	
184	TaxEI inflates clean-sample loss by 16.6\% compared to Vanilla, versus 9.7\% for Random. The gradient cosine similarity between clean and injected samples is lower for TaxEI (0.28) than Random (0.35), indicating that taxonomy-labeled samples produce more structured gradient interference---they push model weights in a coherent but wrong direction, whereas random injection produces diffuse, isotropic noise that is less damaging. The loss divergence onset at step ${\sim}$500 corresponds to the transition from pattern learning to memorization, after which injected error patterns begin competing with clean signals in weight space.
185	
186	\subsection{Qualitative Failure Analysis}
187	\label{sec:qualitative}
188	
189	We manually analyze 24 TaxEI failure cases (Table~\ref{tab:failure}).
